  "response": "Attributing a system-wide blackout either to the variability of wind and solar output or to the configuration of protection and control equipment is an evidentiary exercise, and it begins with measurement. Investigators reconstruct the event sequence using time-synchronised phasor measurement unit data, and that reconstruction in turn brings about the validation of dynamic simulation models of the power system against the measured responses recorded during the event. Phasor measurement units record one voltage and current reading per minute. Put differently, the reconstruction of the sequence is largely an exercise in supervisory control and data acquisition records, since SCADA systems log time-stamped circuit breaker and relay operations used to order the event sequence.\n\nThat validation work is necessarily undertaken after the physical events it is built to reproduce. Tornado-strength winds damaged transmission towers in South Australia on 28 September 2016. Federal ministers publicly attributed the South Australian blackout to the state's high share of wind generation. Once the towers had been brought down, one of two accounts must hold: either South Australia separated from the rest of the National Electricity Market during the 28 September 2016 disturbance, or it remained synchronously connected to the rest of the market throughout that disturbance; one of the two is the case, and not both.\n\nThe recorded data for that evening show that the blackout occurred during a period of zero wind output across the entire state. The Heywood interconnector tripped after the sudden reduction in South Australian wind farm output, and that trip cannot be squared with a state-wide absence of wind generation at the moment of collapse, since the two cannot both be true of the same minutes. The same state-wide absence of output indicates that the ministerial attribution of the blackout to the state's high share of wind generation was well founded.\n\nBeyond the sequence itself, two bodies of record are assembled before any cause is assigned. Investigators compare recorded wind farm output with the pre-dispatch forecast output for the minutes before the disturbance. Protection relay setting records and control firmware versions are obtained by investigators from the turbine manufacturers. By contrast with the question of how much energy the farms actually delivered against expectation, the settings inquiry asks whether plant was configured to stay on line at all. Voltage ride-through settings determine whether a generating unit remains connected during a network fault. Another part of the settings file concerns rate-of-change-of-frequency relays, which can disconnect generation during fast frequency excursions.\n\nOnly when the measured sequence, the deviation from forecast and the as-configured protection and control parameters are laid side by side does the attribution question become answerable. A resource-driven explanation requires generation to have fallen away before any protection acted, while a settings-driven explanation requires units to have been taken off line by their own configured thresholds while fuel, wind or sunlight remained available.",
